void send_fog(bplid pid, color color) ->
Sets the client's fog color.

void send_player_update(bplid pid) ->
Sets the position and orientation of all other players to match the server's.
Sent with send_packet_unreliable().

void send_grenade(bplid pid, fvec3 pos, fvec3 vel, float fuse, plid from) ->
Spawns a grenade.
Physics out of bounds varies by client; OpenSpades treats all out-of-bounds voxels as nonsolid, Voxlap and BetterSpades handle them modulo 512 along the x and y axes.

void send_reload(bplid pid, unsigned mag, unsigned reserve, plid from) ->
When <var>from</var> is not the receiving client, signifies the start of a player's gun reload, usually with a sound effect.
This effect is purely cosmetic; for example, the original server only sends this if the receiving player is determined to be close enough to <var>from</var>.
When <var>from</var> <em>is</em> the receiving client, signifies the end of a gun reload (if applicable) and sets the client's ammo.

void send_map_start(plid pid, unsigned size) ->
Switches the client to its ``map transfer mode''.
Unjoins all joined players. Removes all grenades.
<var>size</var> is nominally the map's zlib stream's size in bytes, but in practice can (should?) be ignored.

void send_map(bplid pid) ->
Calls send_map_start() and send_packet() with lots of MapChunk packets.
Over the wire, an ordinary zlib-compressed Voxlap5 VXL file representing the current map's contents is sent.
<!-- /* TODO: should the iterator be moved to send_state? */ -->
Calls send_state() for each player in the given broadcast pid.

void send_state(plid pid) ->
<strong>This one takes a regular pid, not a broadcast pid.</strong>
Calls send_connected_players().
Calls demand_fingerprint() if this is the first time state was sent to <var>pid</var> since connecting.
Calls send_state_ctf().
If this is the first time state has been sent since connecting, calls demand_fingerprint().
Exits ``map transfer mode'' if in that mode.
Sets what player ID the client should use.
Sets fog color.
Sets team colors and names.
Sets assorted gamemode-related things.

void send_existing_player(bplid pid, unsigned team, unsigned gun, unsigned tool, unsigned score, color blockColor, string name, plid from) ->
Silently joins and spawns a player. Where exactly it spawns is uncertain<!-- and on my TODO list -->.

void send_spawn_player(bplid pid, fvec3 pos, unsigned gun, nteamid team, string name, plid from) ->
Spawns a player. If the player wasn't joined before, joins it now.

<!-- TODO: actually support nil, then add another BSbug for the fun of it -->
<p>If the player was already joined, name may be <kbd>nil</kbd> -- this leaves the name unchanged, unless you're BetterSpades, which is full of bugs.
The original ``Voxlap'' client does not support changing name after join -- new names are ignored.
OpenSpades treats an empty string the same as nil.

<p>The player's orientation is team-dependent, with the first team facing <code>{x=1,y=0,z=0}</code> and the second team facing <code>{x=-1,y=0,z=0}</code>.

<p>If <var>from</var> refers to the receiving player, that player's HP is set to 100, blocks to 50, grenades to 3, and mag/reserve ammo to a protocol version-defined constant:
