void send_chat(bplid pid, string msg, unsigned type, plid from) ->
Displays a chat message.
If <var>type</var>&le;<kbd>1</kbd> and <var>from</var> is joined, displays as if that player sent it; otherwise displays as if the server sent it.
<var>type</var> can be one of the following:
<dl class=num>
<dt><kbd>0</kbd><dd>Global
<dt><kbd>1</kbd><dd>Team
<dt><kbd>2</kbd><dd>Server
</dl>
<p>If <var>from</var> is a non-joined player or <var>type</var>=<kbd>2</kbd>, OpenSpades matches the start of the message against a set of magic strings and changes the message's display accordingly:
<dl>
<dt><kbd><code>"N% "</code></kbd><dd>``Notice''
<dt><kbd><code>"!% "</code></kbd><dd>``Error''
<dt><kbd><code>"%% "</code></kbd><dd>``Warning''
<dt><kbd><code>"C% "</code></kbd><dd>``Center''
</dl>
<p>Additionally, BetterSpades has decided to implement its own unecessary standard for <var>type</var> incompatible with all the other clients:
<!-- TODO: rm this whenever you remember it: <dl><dt>``Big'' (center, red)<dd>3<dt>``Info'' (center, white)<dd>4<dt>``Warning'' (center, yellow)<dd>5<dt>``Error'' (center, red)<dd>6</dl> -->
<dl class=num>
<dt><kbd>3</kbd><dd>``Big'' (center, red)
<dt><kbd>4</kbd><dd>``Info'' (center, white)
<dt><kbd>5</kbd><dd>``Warning'' (center, yellow)
<dt><kbd>6</kbd><dd>``Error'' (center, red)
</dl>
<p>BetterSpades has also decided to add special meaning to use of both <var>from</var>=<kbd>255</kbd> and <var>type</var>=<kbd>2</kbd> together despite their (intuitive?) use in the original server.
These messages are not displayed in chat.
If the client has been connected for at least 1 second, the last message meeting those criteria overrides the disconnect reason which would otherwise be derived from ENet's disconnect data integer.<!-- I think? -->
The reason is limited to 16 octets in length (excluding NUL terminator).
<p>OpenSpades handles the message specially when <var>from</var> is a non-joined player (<var>type</var> is ignored):
<dl>
<dt><kbd><code>"Game mode: Arena by Yourself"</code></kbd><dd><samp>Enabled clearCorpseOnRespawn based on a server message heuristics</samp> [sic]
</dl>
<p>Similarly for IV of Spades:
<dl>
<dt><kbd><code>"This server supports the PosUpgrade extension"</code></kbd><dd><samp>Enabled posUpgradeExtension based on a server message heuristics</samp> [sic]
<dt><kbd><code>"PosUpgrade extension could not be detected"</code></kbd> [sic]<dd><samp>Disabled posUpgradeExtension based on a server message heuristics</samp> [sic]
</dl>

void send_disconnect(bplid pid, plid from) ->
Unjoins a joined player. Makes no distinction between player-initiated and non-player-initiated disconnects.

void send_block_action(plid pid, ivec3 pos, unsigned type, plid from) ->
Places or removes a set of blocks centered around the given position.
<var>type</var> can be one of the following:
<!-- TODO: should i expose macros for these or something? -->
<dl class=num>
<dt><kbd>0</kbd><dd>Build 1 (block)
<dt><kbd>1</kbd><dd>Destroy 1 (spade/gun)
<dt><kbd>2</kbd><dd>Destroy 1x1x3 (spade)
<dt><kbd>3</kbd><dd>Destroy 3x3x3 (grenade, silent)
</dl>

<p>If building, uses the held block color of <var>from</var>.
OpenSpades decreases its number of available blocks by 1 if <var>type</var>=<kbd>0</kbd>. If that would bring the number below 0, don't decrease anything.
OpenSpades increases its number of available blocks by 1 if <var>type</var>=<kbd>1</kbd>. If that would bring the number above 50, don't increase anything.

<p>Block actions<!-- TODO: shitty name --> are batched per frame in an extra-flimsy manner. (E.g. sending a build then destroy that get received on the same frame will process destroy first, then build, and not process gravity at all, or something cursed like that. . .) Documenting it and working around it is on my TODO list.

